### Why do streams not store elements?

A Stream does not allocate memory to hold elements. It is a description of how to process elements from a source. When you call `stream().filter().map()`, Java builds a chain of operation descriptors. No element is processed until a terminal operation triggers the pipeline.

### How does pull-based execution work?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;

public class PullBasedDemo {
    record Transaction(String id, BigDecimal amount, boolean settled) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Transaction> txns = List.of(
            new Transaction("TXN-001", new BigDecimal("5000"), false),
            new Transaction("TXN-002", new BigDecimal("45000"), true),
            new Transaction("TXN-003", new BigDecimal("78000"), true)
        );

        // findFirst pulls only until it gets ONE result
        Optional<BigDecimal> first = txns.stream()
            .peek(t -> System.out.println("Source pulled: " + t.id()))
            .filter(Transaction::settled)
            .peek(t -> System.out.println("Filter passed: " + t.id()))
            .map(Transaction::amount)
            .findFirst();

        System.out.println("Result: Rs." + first.orElse(BigDecimal.ZERO));
    }
}
```

```text
Source pulled: TXN-001
Source pulled: TXN-002
Filter passed: TXN-002
Result: Rs.45000
```

TXN-003 was never pulled from the source because findFirst stopped after receiving the first matching element.

### What is loop fusion?

Multiple stateless operations execute in a single pass per element, not separate passes per operation.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.List;

public class FusionDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List.of("SAV2001", "SAV2002", "CUR3001").stream()
            .peek(a -> System.out.println("1-filter: " + a))
            .filter(a -> a.startsWith("SAV"))
            .peek(a -> System.out.println("2-map: " + a))
            .map(String::toLowerCase)
            .forEach(a -> System.out.println("3-result: " + a));
    }
}
```

```text
1-filter: SAV2001
2-map: SAV2001
3-result: sav2001
1-filter: SAV2002
2-map: SAV2002
3-result: sav2002
1-filter: CUR3001
```

Each element goes through all stages before the next element is pulled. CUR3001 was dropped by filter, so map and result never see it.

### What about stateful operations?

Stateless operations (filter, map, flatMap, peek) process one element at a time with O(1) memory. Stateful operations must buffer:

| Operation | Stateful? | Memory | Notes |
|-----------|-----------|--------|-------|
| filter | No | O(1) | Checks one element independently |
| map | No | O(1) | Transforms one element |
| sorted | Yes | O(n) | Buffers entire stream before sorting |
| distinct | Yes | O(n) | Maintains HashSet of seen elements |
| limit | Yes | O(1) | Counter, short-circuits |

### How do infinite streams work?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class InfiniteStreamDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Infinite source + short-circuit = finite result
        List<String> ids = Stream.iterate(1, n -> n + 1)
            .map(n -> "TXN-" + String.format("%06d", n))
            .filter(id -> id.endsWith("5"))  // every 10th
            .limit(3)                          // stop after 3
            .toList();

        System.out.println(ids);
    }
}
```

```text
[TXN-000005, TXN-000015, TXN-000025]
```

The infinite source generates elements forever, but limit(3) stops the pipeline after three matches. Only 25 elements were ever generated.

### What are the performance implications?

Filter before sorted reduces the number of elements that need buffering. Limit before sorted is pointless because sorted must see all elements anyway. Place stateless operations (filter, map) early in the pipeline and stateful operations (sorted, distinct) later.

### What should a senior engineer know?

The Spliterator is the low-level engine that drives stream traversal. tryAdvance pulls one element, forEachRemaining processes all remaining, and trySplit divides for parallel processing. Understanding Spliterator helps when creating custom stream sources or debugging performance issues.
